(window.SITE_PARTS=window.SITE_PARTS||{})["bill-prosperity"]={"plain":{"bills":{"prosperity":{"slug":"prosperity","title":"The People's Prosperity Act","sections":[{"anchor":"sec-0-1","heading":"\u00a7 0-1. Short Title","part":"SECTION 0 \u2014 SHORT TITLE, FINDINGS, AND DEFINITIONS","body":"This Act may be cited as the People's Prosperity Act.","partHeads":[{"heading":"SECTION 0 \u2014 SHORT TITLE, FINDINGS, AND DEFINITIONS","body":""}]},{"anchor":"sec-0-2","heading":"\u00a7 0-2. Findings","part":"SECTION 0 \u2014 SHORT TITLE, FINDINGS, AND DEFINITIONS","body":"Congress finds the following:\n\n1. Real earnings for workers in the middle of the earnings distribution have grown slowly and unevenly over decades, while the instruments by which household wealth is ordinarily built \u2014 diversified, low-cost, long-horizon investment accounts \u2014 remain unavailable or impractical for a large share of working people.\n\n2. The Federal Government already operates, for its own employees, a retirement investment system of exceptional quality and cost: a small menu of broad index funds at expenses far below those available in the retail market. There is no reason of principle why access to that system should depend on being employed by the Government.\n\n3. A person who has no savings will not lock money away that cannot be reached in an emergency. Access, low cost, easy enrollment, hardship access, and protection from seizure are conditions of saving, not amenities.\n\n4. A published, methodologically fixed measure of middle-class earnings serves the public interest independently of any use made of it, and its publication is itself a public good.\n\n4A. A subsidy conditioned on an affirmative act is claimed most reliably by those with the time, attention, and administrative capacity to claim it. Where a matching contribution for retirement saving has been offered on that basis, participation has concentrated among households already saving, and the subsidy has flowed least to the households for whom it was designed. Enrollment by default, with an immediate and costless opt-out, is the condition on which a matching contribution reaches the households named in these findings rather than the households best positioned to claim it.\n\n4A-1. These propositions have been tested. A randomized trial begun in 2007 assigned a representative sample of Oklahoma newborns to receive an automatically opened account with an initial deposit, and followed them for seventeen years. Among the first participants to reach the funds for postsecondary study, use did not vary significantly by race or ethnicity, by poverty status, by the mother's education, or by the language spoken at home. An account opened for a person, without application, is used by that person whatever their circumstances; an account that must be sought is not.\n\n4B. Universality is a condition of durability. A program that reaches every person creates the constituency that sustains it; a program that reaches only those who seek it out has neither the coverage nor the defenders to survive an ordinary appropriations cycle.\n\n5. **Enumerated powers.** Congress enacts the statistical provisions of Section 1 pursuant to Article I, Section 8, Clause 1, and Article I, Section 8, Clause 18, as necessary and proper to the exercise of the legislative power. Congress enacts Sections 2 and 3 pursuant to Article I, Section 8, Clause 1, the power to lay and collect taxes and to provide for the general welfare, and Article I, Section 8, Clause 2, the power to borrow money on the credit of the United States. The tax provisions are enacted pursuant to the Sixteenth Amendment."},{"anchor":"sec-0-3","heading":"\u00a7 0-3. Definitions","part":"SECTION 0 \u2014 SHORT TITLE, FINDINGS, AND DEFINITIONS","body":"For purposes of this Act:\n\n**(a) \"The Middle-Class Growth Rate\"** has the meaning given in \u00a7 1-1.\n\n**(b) \"The Board\"** means the Federal Retirement Thrift Investment Board.\n\n**(c) \"The Secretary\"** means the Secretary of the Treasury.\n\n**(d) \"Eligible individual\"** means an individual holding a Social Security number or an individual taxpayer identification number who has income from labor as described in \u00a7 3-1(c). Any eligible individual may hold and contribute to a Prosperity Account. The federal match and seed under \u00a7 3-6, the government share of the default contribution under \u00a7 3-6A, and the newborn seed under \u00a7 3-3(b), are available only to an eligible individual who is a citizen of the United States, without regard to income-tax liability.\n\n**(e) \"Prosperity Account\"** means an account established under Section 3.\n\n**(f) \"The Fund\"** means the People's Prosperity Fund established under \u00a7 2-1.\n\n**(g) \"Federal means-tested program\"** means a program of the United States, or a program of a State administered with Federal funds, **under which eligibility for a benefit, or the amount of a benefit, is determined in whole or in part by the income or the resources of the applicant or of the applicant's household.** The term includes the programs named in \u00a7 3-9(b)(1) and (2) and every program of like character. **The omission of a program from that enumeration is not a determination that it falls outside this definition.**\n\n**(g-1) That term is defined here and not borrowed.** The meaning given in subsection (g) **does not depend on the definition of any similar term in any other statute, or on any construction given to such a term** \u2014 including a term defined for the purpose of restricting eligibility by reason of immigration status."},{"anchor":"sec-0-4","heading":"\u00a7 0-4. Relationship to Existing Law","part":"SECTION 0 \u2014 SHORT TITLE, FINDINGS, AND DEFINITIONS","body":"**(a) Floor, not ceiling.** No provision of this Act reduces any benefit, protection, or entitlement of existing law. Where existing law is more favorable to a person, it continues to apply.\n\n**(a-1) No employer obligation beyond facilitation.** As provided in \u00a7 3-2(c), an employer's sole obligation under this Act is to forward a payroll deduction and transmit a notice. This Act imposes no contribution obligation, no fiduciary duty, and no plan-sponsorship obligation on any employer.\n\n**(b) Independence from the integrity Acts.** This Act imposes no duty on any officeholder or candidate and confers no authority on any body established by the Executive Integrity Act, the Congressional Integrity Act, the Judiciary Integrity Act, or the Candidate Integrity Act. Section 2 defines an instrument those Acts require certain persons to hold; the requirement is imposed by those Acts and not by this one. Where any of those Acts refers to the Middle-Class Growth Rate, to the Fund, or to a fund offered under Section 3, the reference is to this Act as a source of definition only, and no provision of this Act is conditioned on the enactment or continued effect of any of them."},{"anchor":"sec-1-1","heading":"\u00a7 1-1. The Middle-Class Growth Rate","part":"SECTION 1 \u2014 THE MIDDLE-CLASS GROWTH RATE AND THE STATISTICS OF MIDDLE-CLASS EARNINGS","body":"**(a) Definition.** The Middle-Class Growth Rate is the sum of:\n\n1. the **wage term**: the average annual growth in nominal hourly wages of workers between the 21st and 80th percentiles of the individual hourly wage distribution, composition-adjusted under subsection (c), over the ten years ending with the measurement year; and\n\n2. the **employment term**: 1.5 multiplied by the average annual change, expressed in percentage points, in prime-age full-time-equivalent hours per capita as defined in subsection (d), over the three years ending with the measurement year.\n\nThe two terms are added. Neither is multiplied by the other, and neither is conditioned on the sign of the other.\n\n**(a-1) The measurement year.** The measurement year for a certification under \u00a7 1-3(a) is the calendar year preceding the calendar year in which falls the date published for that certification under \u00a7 1-3(a), whether or not the certification is made on that date. The Bureau shall fix each such date on a day by which the vintage of data specified for it contains the data for every month of the measurement year.\n\n**(b) Nominal, not real.** The wage term measures nominal wage growth. The Middle-Class Growth Rate therefore rises with inflation to the extent that middle-class wages rise with inflation, and no further.\n\n**(c) Composition adjustment.** The wage term shall be computed on a matched-sample or fixed-weight basis, so that it measures the change in wages of the same workers or of a population of constant composition, and not the change in the composition of the workforce.\n\n**(c-1) Which basis applies.** The wage term is computed on a fixed-weight basis over the ten-year window under subsection (a)(1). Matched-sample computation applies only within a period for which the source series supports matching of the same respondents, and the Bureau shall publish the periods for which it does.\n\n**(c-2) The weights.** The weights used in a fixed-weight computation are those of the base period and are fixed under \u00a7 1-2. A change to those weights is an amendment to the methodology of the Middle-Class Growth Rate and may be made only as \u00a7 1-2 provides.\n\n**(d) The employment measure.** Prime-age full-time-equivalent hours per capita is the total hours worked by all persons aged 25 through 54, counting each individual's hours up to a maximum of 40 per week and disregarding hours above 40, divided by the number of persons aged 25 through 54.\n\n**(e) Symmetry.** The Middle-Class Growth Rate has no floor. Where the measured wages or hours decline, it declines, and where the sum is negative, it is negative.","partHeads":[{"heading":"SECTION 1 \u2014 THE MIDDLE-CLASS GROWTH RATE AND THE STATISTICS OF MIDDLE-CLASS EARNINGS","body":""}]},{"anchor":"sec-1-2","heading":"\u00a7 1-2. Methodology Lock","part":"SECTION 1 \u2014 THE MIDDLE-CLASS GROWTH RATE AND THE STATISTICS OF MIDDLE-CLASS EARNINGS","body":"**(a) What is locked.** The population, definitions, methodology, source series, percentile band, hours cap, employment coefficient, and window lengths of the Middle-Class Growth Rate defined in \u00a7 1-1 are fixed as of the date of enactment. Any amendment to any of them requires **a two-thirds vote of each House**, a recorded roll-call vote, and an effective date no earlier than three years after enactment of the amendment.\n\n**(a-0) Basis and limits.** Whether a statute may bind the procedure by which a future Congress amends it is not settled. A court that declines to treat subsection (a)'s vote requirement as binding leaves \u00a7 1-1 in force as ordinary law, unaffected by that holding, unless and until amended by whatever vote is otherwise sufficient \u2014 and the three-year effective-date rule, which binds no future vote, continues to apply to any amendment enacted. This subsection does not purport to bind any future Congress beyond what Article I permits.\n\n**(a-1) Succession of a source series.** What subsection (a) locks as to methodology is the concept measured \u2014 the definition, population, percentile band, hours cap, employment coefficient, and window lengths. Where a source series is discontinued, redefined, or replaced by the agency producing it in the ordinary course of statistical maintenance, the Bureau shall use the successor series, shall publish a crosswalk and a bridged back-series, and shall certify that the substitution does not change the concept measured. A substitution made under this subsection is not an amendment for purposes of subsection (a).\n\n**(a-2) Conditions on a substitution.** A substitution under subsection (a-1) takes effect only after the Bureau publishes the crosswalk, the bridged series, and both the prior and the successor computations for a period during which the two overlap. A substitution for which the certification required by subsection (a-1) is not published does not take effect. Where a locked source series has been discontinued and no substitution has taken effect, the Bureau shall publish that fact, the reason, and the date by which it expects to publish the certification, and shall renew that publication every 90 days until the substitution takes effect. This subsection conditions the substitution; it does not suspend the duty to certify under \u00a7 1-3.\n\n**(a-3) Redesigns pending at enactment.** A redesign of a source series announced before the date of enactment is governed by subsection (a-1)."},{"anchor":"sec-1-2a","heading":"\u00a7 1-2A. Decadal Review of the Employment Coefficient","part":"SECTION 1 \u2014 THE MIDDLE-CLASS GROWTH RATE AND THE STATISTICS OF MIDDLE-CLASS EARNINGS","body":"**(a)** The Bureau shall publish, not less than every ten years beginning ten years after enactment, an assessment of the employment coefficient in \u00a7 1-1(a)(2), stating whether subsequent recession and recovery episodes support, contradict, or are inconclusive as to the coefficient's calibration, and what coefficient the data would now support.\n\n**(b)** The review is advisory. It does not alter the Middle-Class Growth Rate, which may be amended only as \u00a7 1-2 provides."},{"anchor":"sec-1-3","heading":"\u00a7 1-3. Certification; the Statistic and the Peg Distinguished","part":"SECTION 1 \u2014 THE MIDDLE-CLASS GROWTH RATE AND THE STATISTICS OF MIDDLE-CLASS EARNINGS","body":"**(a) Annual certification.** The Bureau of Labor Statistics shall determine and certify the Middle-Class Growth Rate once each year, on a fixed date published not less than one year in advance, using a specified vintage of the underlying data.\n\n**(a-1) The wage term certified with it.** With each certification under subsection (a), the Bureau shall certify the wage term under \u00a7 1-1(a)(1) computed for that certification. The wage term so certified is part of the certification of the Middle-Class Growth Rate for every purpose of this Act, and subsections (b) and (d) apply to it as they apply to the Middle-Class Growth Rate.\n\n**(b) The certified value is not revised.** The value certified under subsection (a) governs any compensation, crediting, or other consequence that any law attaches to the Middle-Class Growth Rate, and is not altered by any later revision of the underlying data. No consequence attaching to a certified value is reopened, restated, or recovered by reason of a revision.\n\n**(c) The statistics are revised.** The series published under \u00a7 1-4 are estimates and shall be revised as data improve. Revision of a statistic is the ordinary operation of statistical practice and is not a defect.\n\n**(d) Publish the divergence.** With each certification, the Bureau shall publish the certified value, the Bureau's current best estimate of the same quantity, and the difference between them.\n\n**(e) Label.** The Middle-Class Growth Rate is a metric for compensation and disclosure. It is not a measure of wage inflation, is not a target for monetary policy, and is constructed differently from the Employment Cost Index, average hourly earnings, and other wage measures with which it will diverge. The Bureau shall publish it with a statement to that effect."},{"anchor":"sec-1-4","heading":"\u00a7 1-4. Publication Program","part":"SECTION 1 \u2014 THE MIDDLE-CLASS GROWTH RATE AND THE STATISTICS OF MIDDLE-CLASS EARNINGS","body":"**(a) Purpose.** The series required by this Section are published for the information of the public and for the making of economic policy, independently of any use made of the Middle-Class Growth Rate. Their value does not depend on that use.\n\n**(b) Series and frequency.** The Bureau shall collect and publish, in structured, machine-readable, bulk-downloadable form:\n\n1. **Monthly** \u2014 prime-age employment and prime-age full-time-equivalent hours per capita.\n\n2. **Quarterly** \u2014 hourly wages at each percentile from the 1st through the 100th, on a three-month moving average basis.\n\n3. **Annually** \u2014 the composition-adjusted wage series with full demographic and occupational controls; the price index under paragraph (5); the labor share, capital share, and corporate profit share of national income; household debt service as a share of income for the measured band; and the certified Middle-Class Growth Rate and the certified wage term under \u00a7 1-3(a-1), each with the disclosures required by \u00a7 1-3(d).\n\n4. **Annually** \u2014 hours worked per prime-age person at each percentile from the 1st through the 100th, including hours above 40, so that involuntary part-time work and overwork are each visible.\n\n5. **Annually** \u2014 a price index weighted to the expenditure basket of households between the 21st and 80th percentiles, decomposed into housing, healthcare, childcare, transportation, and food, each decomposed category published as a series in its own right. The childcare category shall be published as a separate headline series, released on its own line and not solely as a component of the composite index.\n\n6. **Annually** \u2014 unpaid care hours per prime-age person, measured from the American Time Use Survey, disaggregated by sex and by household composition, published alongside the paid-hours distribution under paragraph (4).\n\n**(c) Uncertainty published with every estimate.** Each series under subsection (b), and the certified Middle-Class Growth Rate, shall be published with its standard error and a confidence interval.\n\n**(d) Disaggregation.** The series under subsection (b)(1), (2), and (4) shall be published by census division, sex, race and ethnicity, educational attainment, and broad industry, at the frequency the sample supports, under published suppression rules.\n\n**(d-1) The sample required.** For each disaggregation required by subsection (d), the Bureau shall publish the sample size required to support it at a stated level of precision, and, where the current sample does not support it, a plan and schedule to reach that sample.\n\n**(d-2) Suppression is published, not silent.** Where a cell is suppressed under the suppression rules applicable to subsection (d), the Bureau shall publish the fact of the suppression and the number of cells suppressed, so that the gap is visible rather than absent.\n\n**(e) Replicability.** The Bureau shall publish the methodology of each series in sufficient detail to permit reproduction, including the matching algorithm, composition-adjustment weights, treatment of imputed and topcoded values, and treatment of missing observations, and shall make the underlying microdata available to researchers under the confidentiality protections applicable to its other restricted-access data.\n\n**(f) International comparability.** The Bureau shall adopt definitions harmonized with those of the Organisation for Economic Co-operation and Development where practicable, and shall publish a crosswalk where not."},{"anchor":"sec-1-5","heading":"\u00a7 1-5. Release Integrity","part":"SECTION 1 \u2014 THE MIDDLE-CLASS GROWTH RATE AND THE STATISTICS OF MIDDLE-CLASS EARNINGS","body":"**(a) Fixed calendar.** The Bureau shall publish, not less than one year in advance, the date and time of each release under this Section, and shall adhere to it.\n\n**(b) No clearance.** A release under this Section is not subject to review, approval, or clearance by the Secretary of Labor, by any officer of the Department, or by any officer outside the Bureau, as to its content, its timing, or its accompanying analysis.\n\n**(c) Responsible official.** A career professional employee of the Bureau shall be responsible for the content of each release.\n\n**(d) Changes published with reasons.** Any change to the release calendar, and any delay in a release, shall be published with a statement of the reason, at the time of the change."},{"anchor":"sec-1-5a","heading":"\u00a7 1-5A. Independence of the Officials Responsible for the Statistic","part":"SECTION 1 \u2014 THE MIDDLE-CLASS GROWTH RATE AND THE STATISTICS OF MIDDLE-CLASS EARNINGS","body":"**(a) Purpose.** \u00a7 1-5 protects the release. This Section protects the officials who produce it. A methodology fixed under \u00a7 1-2 and a release insulated under \u00a7 1-5 are of no effect if the persons who compute and certify the statistic serve at the pleasure of those whose compensation it sets.\n\n**(b) Term.** The Commissioner of Labor Statistics shall be appointed for a term of the length stated in the schedule to this Act, and may continue to serve after the expiration of that term until a successor has taken office.\n\n**(c) Procedural conditions on removal.** The removal of the Commissioner is effective only upon \u2014\n\n**(1)** a written statement, signed by the President, of the specific and case-related grounds for the removal;\n\n**(2)** delivery of that statement to the Commissioner, and an opportunity to respond in writing within the period stated in the schedule to this Act; and\n\n**(3)** transmission of the statement, and of any response, to the Committees of the Congress having jurisdiction, and publication of both.\n\n**(d) Grounds.** The Commissioner may be removed only for inefficiency, neglect of duty, or malfeasance in office. Whether a stated ground complies with this subsection is a question for a court.\n\n**(e) The responsible official under \u00a7 1-5(c).** An adverse action taken against the career professional employee designated under \u00a7 1-5(c), within the period stated in the schedule to this Act after a release for which that employee was responsible, is effective only upon a written statement of the grounds for the action, signed by the officer taking it, published and transmitted to the Committees of the Congress having jurisdiction.\n\n**(f) No content review.** Nothing in this Section subjects the content, timing, or methodology of any release to review by any officer outside the Bureau, and no ground stated under subsection (c) or (e) may consist of disagreement with the content of a release, with a series required by \u00a7 1-4, or with the certified Middle-Class Growth Rate.\n\n**(g) Severability within this Section.** Subsections (c), (e) and (f) are severable from subsections (b) and (d) and from each other. If subsection (b) or (d) is held invalid, or invalid as applied, subsections (c), (e) and (f) continue in effect, and the Commissioner remains removable subject to the procedural conditions they impose. Congress would have enacted subsections (c), (e) and (f) independently of any limitation on the grounds of removal.\n\n**(h) No tenure otherwise conferred.** Nothing in this Section confers tenure in office beyond what it provides, requires that any person be reinstated, or limits removal upon grounds stated in conformity with this Section."},{"anchor":"sec-1-6","heading":"\u00a7 1-6. Resources","part":"SECTION 1 \u2014 THE MIDDLE-CLASS GROWTH RATE AND THE STATISTICS OF MIDDLE-CLASS EARNINGS","body":"**(a) Authorization.** There are authorized to be appropriated to the Bureau of Labor Statistics, for each fiscal year, such sums as are necessary to carry out this Section, including: expansion of the Current Population Survey sample to the size required to support percentile estimates at the disaggregation required by \u00a7 1-4(d); the construction and maintenance of the composition-adjusted series; the construction and maintenance of the price index under \u00a7 1-4(b)(5), including the separate childcare headline series required by that paragraph; the construction and maintenance of the unpaid-care-hours series under \u00a7 1-4(b)(6), including any expansion of the American Time Use Survey necessary to support its required disaggregation; the publication, storage, and bulk distribution of every series; the restricted-access microdata facility under \u00a7 1-4(e); and the periodic methodological review under \u00a7 1-7.\n\n**(a-1) Permanent appropriation for the peg.** There are appropriated, out of any money in the Treasury not otherwise appropriated, such sums as are necessary to produce **the certified Middle-Class Growth Rate and the series necessary to compute it**, at the precision and disaggregation this Section requires. Amounts under this subsection are appropriated permanently and indefinitely, are available without further appropriation and without fiscal-year limitation, and do not lapse.\n\n**(b) Sample, not only publication.** The authorization under subsection (a) is for the collection of data as well as its publication.\n\n**(c) No obligation without appropriation.** **Except for the duties funded by subsection (a-1), which are effective in every fiscal year without regard to this subsection**, no duty imposed by this Section is effective in a fiscal year except to the extent that amounts are appropriated to carry it out. Where amounts appropriated are insufficient, the Bureau shall produce the series in the order of priority in subsection (d), shall publish a statement identifying each series not produced and the amount required to produce it, and shall transmit that statement to the Congress. No penalty, finding, or consequence of any kind attaches to the Bureau or to any officer or employee for a series not produced for want of appropriation.\n\n**(c-1) Methodological degradation.** Where amounts appropriated are sufficient to produce a series but not at the precision or disaggregation this Section requires, the Bureau shall produce the series at the precision the appropriation supports, shall publish a statement identifying the shortfall and its effect on reliability, and shall not withhold a series because it cannot yet be produced at full specification. A series produced under this subsection satisfies the Bureau's obligation under this Section for the fiscal year, and no penalty or consequence attaches to the Bureau for the reduced precision. **A series funded by subsection (a-1) may not be produced under this subsection**, and the certified Middle-Class Growth Rate under \u00a7 1-3 may not be computed from a series so produced. Where any other series used in the certification was produced under this subsection, the certification shall state that fact and identify the methodological limitation.\n\n**(d) Order of priority.** Where amounts appropriated are insufficient to produce every series, the Bureau shall produce them in the following order: first, the certified Middle-Class Growth Rate and the series necessary to compute it; second, the monthly employment and hours series; third, the quarterly percentile wage series; fourth, the annual composition-adjusted series and the price index; fifth, the disaggregations under \u00a7 1-4(d); and sixth, the unpaid-care-hours series under \u00a7 1-4(b)(6) and the context series under \u00a7 1-4(b)(3).\n\n**(e) No displacement of existing work.** Amounts appropriated under this Section are in addition to, and shall not be derived from, amounts otherwise available to the Bureau. The Bureau shall not reduce, delay, or discontinue any series it produced before enactment in order to carry out this Section.\n\n**(f) Annual certification of adequacy.** The Bureau shall certify annually whether the amounts appropriated are sufficient to produce every series required by this Section at the reliability its published standards require, and shall state, where they are not, the amount required and the consequence of the shortfall. The certification is published and transmitted to the Congress.\n\n**(g) Effect on other agencies.** Any obligation imposed by this Section on an agency other than the Bureau is subject to the same conditions, and no such agency is obligated to act in a fiscal year except to the extent that amounts are appropriated to it for the purpose. **This subsection does not reach an obligation to furnish data necessary to produce a series funded by subsection (a-1)**, which is effective without regard to any appropriation to the furnishing agency.\n\n**(h) No-year availability.** Amounts appropriated under subsection (a) are available until expended.\n\n**(i) Continuity during a lapse in appropriations.** The certification of the Middle-Class Growth Rate under \u00a7 1-3, the collection of the data necessary to it, and the release of any series on a date published under \u00a7 1-5(a) are activities authorized by law to continue during a lapse in appropriations, and obligations incurred for them are authorized in advance of appropriations for purposes of section 1342 of title 31, United States Code. The officers and employees necessary to perform them shall be designated as excepted from furlough, identified in the Bureau's shutdown plan, and that identification published."},{"anchor":"sec-1-7","heading":"\u00a7 1-7. Methodological Review","part":"SECTION 1 \u2014 THE MIDDLE-CLASS GROWTH RATE AND THE STATISTICS OF MIDDLE-CLASS EARNINGS","body":"The Bureau shall review the methodology of each series under \u00a7 1-4 not less than every five years, shall publish the review, and shall state whether any series has ceased to measure what it was constructed to measure. A review may not alter the Middle-Class Growth Rate, which is fixed under \u00a7 1-2 and may be amended only as that Section provides."},{"anchor":"sec-2-1","heading":"\u00a7 2-1. Establishment","part":"SECTION 2 \u2014 THE PEOPLE'S PROSPERITY FUND","body":"There is established the People's Prosperity Fund, a nonmarketable special-issue security of the Treasury, credited annually at a rate equal to the Middle-Class Growth Rate as certified under \u00a7 1-3. Where the Middle-Class Growth Rate is negative, the balance is reduced accordingly.","partHeads":[{"heading":"SECTION 2 \u2014 THE PEOPLE'S PROSPERITY FUND","body":""}]},{"anchor":"sec-2-2","heading":"\u00a7 2-2. Availability","part":"SECTION 2 \u2014 THE PEOPLE'S PROSPERITY FUND","body":"**(a) Who may hold.** An interest in the Fund may be held only by a person who is subject to a holding requirement under the Executive Integrity Act, the Congressional Integrity Act, or the Judiciary Integrity Act, or who has made an election under any of those Acts, and by such a person's spouse, domestic partner, or dependent child to the extent that person's assets are attributed under the applicable Act. The Fund is not offered to the public.\n\n**(b) Not a retail instrument.** The Fund is not a savings product, a retirement benefit, a guarantee of return, or a mandatory contribution. Its return is the Middle-Class Growth Rate, which is not adjusted for inflation under \u00a7 1-1(b) and which may be negative. A person seeking a general savings or retirement instrument is served by the Prosperity Account under Section 3, and by the marketable and nonmarketable securities the Secretary otherwise offers.\n\n**(c) Administration.** The Fund is administered by the Secretary. The Secretary shall permit a holding to be maintained within a tax-advantaged retirement account where the applicable Act so requires, and shall set no minimum other than a nominal purchase amount."},{"anchor":"sec-2-3","heading":"\u00a7 2-3. Restrictions on the Interest","part":"SECTION 2 \u2014 THE PEOPLE'S PROSPERITY FUND","body":"An interest in the Fund may not be sold to another person, pledged, assigned, hedged, borrowed against, or timed. It is redeemable only with the Secretary, on terms the Secretary publishes, which shall permit redemption not less frequently than annually and shall permit a withdrawal at any time sufficient to pay the tax on crediting under \u00a7 2-4."},{"anchor":"sec-2-4","heading":"\u00a7 2-4. Tax Treatment","part":"SECTION 2 \u2014 THE PEOPLE'S PROSPERITY FUND","body":"Positive crediting to the Fund is ordinary income to the holder in the year credited, in the manner of original issue discount on a Treasury obligation, and increases the holder's basis in the Fund interest by the amount so credited and taxed, so that the same crediting is not taxed again on distribution. Negative crediting is not currently deductible and instead reduces the holder's basis and the amount of a later taxable distribution. A distribution is taxable only to the extent it exceeds the holder's basis. Where the Fund is held within a Prosperity Account or another tax-advantaged form, the tax rules of that form govern. Outside such a form, the Fund does not receive tax-deferred or tax-exempt treatment, and no step-up in basis under 26 U.S.C. \u00a7 1014 applies to a Fund interest."},{"anchor":"sec-2-5","heading":"\u00a7 2-5. Purpose and Independence","part":"SECTION 2 \u2014 THE PEOPLE'S PROSPERITY FUND","body":"**(a)** The Fund exists so that a person who holds or seeks public office may hold an asset whose value rises only as the earnings of the middle of the earnings distribution rise, and which therefore confers no interest in any particular company, sector, or policy.\n\n**(b)** This Section defines an instrument. It imposes no duty on any person. The requirement to hold the Fund, where it exists, is imposed by the Executive Integrity Act, the Congressional Integrity Act, or the Judiciary Integrity Act, and not by this Act.\n\n**(c)** If none of those Acts is in effect, the Secretary is not required to issue the Fund, and no other provision of this Act is affected."},{"anchor":"sec-3-1","heading":"\u00a7 3-1. The Prosperity Account","part":"SECTION 3 \u2014 THE PROSPERITY ACCOUNT","body":"**(a) A single lifetime account.** There is established a unified system of individual investment accounts, the Prosperity Account, held by a person from birth through working life and into retirement as one continuous account. The Federal Retirement Thrift Investment Board, in coordination with the Secretary of the Treasury, shall administer the system.\n\n**(b) Auto-creation by operation of law.** **An individual holding a Social Security number or an individual taxpayer identification number has a Prosperity Account by operation of this subsection, without any action by the Board and without application.** For an individual who is not a citizen at birth, **the account exists from the moment** the individual is first issued a Social Security number for employment purposes, including upon admission under any visa or status authorizing employment or upon a grant of employment authorization. **The Board shall record and administer each account existing under this subsection. A failure of the Board to record an account, or to receive information about it, does not affect the existence of the account or any right of its holder under subsection (b-1).** The Commissioner of Social Security shall transmit to the Board, on a schedule the Board prescribes, the information necessary to record accounts at issuance; **the schedule governs the Board's records and not the existence of any account.** An account is not created for an individual whose visa or status does not authorize employment; if such an individual later receives employment authorization, an account is created at that time.\n\n**(b-1) What the account provides.** Every account holder, without regard to citizenship, receives: the account itself; access to the fund menu under \u00a7 3-4 at the expense ceiling under subsection (f); portability under subsection (e); the hardship-access and creditor-protection provisions of \u00a7 3-10; and the benefit-neutrality provisions of \u00a7 3-9. A non-citizen account holder contributes from earnings and builds wealth on the same terms as any other worker. The federal match, the federal seed, the newborn seed, and the government share of the default contribution are available only to citizens under \u00a7 0-3(d); all other features of the account are available to every worker.\n\n**(b-2) Voluntary enrollment for lawful residents.** An individual who is lawfully present in the United States but is not issued a Social Security number for employment purposes may request that the Board establish an account for the individual, or for the individual's minor child who is lawfully present. The Board shall establish the account upon verification of lawful presence and issuance of an individual taxpayer identification number. An account established under this subsection is not auto-enrolled under \u00a7 3-2; the holder contributes voluntarily through the channels of \u00a7 3-2(f). The federal match, seed, newborn seed, and government share are not available to an account established under this subsection unless the holder is a citizen of the United States.\n\n**(c) Broad eligibility.** Any individual with income from labor, including wages, salary, self-employment income, and income from independent, contract, gig, or informal work, may contribute to and receive the match under \u00a7 3-6 for a Prosperity Account. Eligibility is not limited to persons with a traditional employer or to persons with income reported on a Form W-2.\n\n**(d) Enrollment by default; opt-out at any time.** An eligible individual is enrolled and contributes through the channel applicable to that individual under \u00a7 3-2, unless the individual declines. An individual may decline before the first contribution, or opt out at any time thereafter, by a single action through any channel the Board provides, effective immediately and without cause, explanation, or penalty. A contribution made within 90 days before an opt-out is returned to the individual on request, with earnings, and any match on a returned contribution is recovered from the account.\n\n**(d-1) The money remains the individual's own.** A contribution under this Act is the property of the account holder from the moment it is made. It is not a tax, a fee, a public fund, or a contribution to any common enterprise. It is held in the holder's own account, invested as the holder directs or in the default under subsection (g), and withdrawable as this Act provides.\n\n**(e) Portability.** An account is portable across employers, periods without employment, and stages of life. It is not closed or converted by reason of a change in employment or the attainment of any age.\n\n**(f) Fee ceiling and pooled administration.** The all-in expense of an account shall not exceed a ceiling, expressed in basis points, set in the schedule to this Act. The Board shall administer the accounts through pooled recordkeeping on the infrastructure by which it administers the Thrift Savings Plan, so that the marginal administrative cost of an additional account approaches zero. The Board shall not establish a standalone retail product for the accounts.\n\n**(g) Default investment and tax character.** An account holder who makes no affirmative investment election is invested in the lifecycle fund corresponding to the account holder's age. A contribution made by default under \u00a7 3-2 is made on an after-tax basis, in the manner of a Roth contribution, unless the account holder elects otherwise.\n\n**(h) Alternative option available on request.** The Board shall offer, and any account holder may elect at any time on request, an alternative investment consisting of a non-interest-bearing obligation of the United States or an equivalent cash-preservation vehicle bearing no interest. The Board shall not require a reason for the election, shall not inquire into the account holder's reason, and shall not require documentation. An account holder electing the alternative option retains every seed and match for which the holder is otherwise eligible, and forfeits nothing by the election.","partHeads":[{"heading":"SECTION 3 \u2014 THE PROSPERITY ACCOUNT","body":""}]},{"anchor":"sec-3-2","heading":"\u00a7 3-2. Automatic Enrollment","part":"SECTION 3 \u2014 THE PROSPERITY ACCOUNT","body":"**(a) Three channels, matched to circumstance.** Each eligible individual is enrolled through the channel applicable to that individual:\n\n1. **An individual with an employer** is enrolled through payroll deduction at the default rate set in the schedule to this Act, forwarded by the employer under subsection (c). Where a government share of the default contribution is deposited under \u00a7 3-6A, the amount deducted from the individual's pay is the worker's share under that Section, which the employer forwards under subsection (c), and the government share is deposited by the Secretary.\n\n2. **An individual who files a Federal income-tax return and has no employer forwarding deductions** is enrolled through the diversion of a portion of any refund, at the default share set in the schedule, elected on the return with a box to decline.\n\n3. **An individual who neither has an employer nor files a return** is enrolled by the creation of the account and the deposit of the seed under \u00a7 3-6(d), with no contribution defaulted.\n\n**(b) Federal channels primary.** The Secretary shall identify individuals described in paragraph (a)(3) from information returns filed by third parties and from other records held by the Secretary, and shall create accounts and deposit seeds from those records without any action by any State. The channels in subsection (d) supplement this mechanism.\n\n**(b-1) Coverage measured and published.** The Secretary shall publish annually an estimate of the eligible population not enrolled, and the channels through which the unenrolled were sought.\n\n**(b-2) Records that hold the unenrolled.** For the sole purpose of creating an account and depositing a seed under this Section, the Secretary may obtain identifying information from the Commissioner of Social Security and from any Federal agency administering a benefit program. Information obtained under this subsection shall be used for that purpose only, shall not be disclosed for any other purpose, and shall not be used in any determination adverse to any person. A disclosure to the Secretary under this subsection is authorized notwithstanding section 552a of title 5, United States Code, and section 6103 of the Internal Revenue Code; this subsection establishes no routine use for any other purpose and authorizes no disclosure by the Secretary.\n\n**(c) Employer facilitation only; no plan sponsorship.** An employer's sole obligation under this Act is to forward a payroll deduction to the Board and to transmit the notice under \u00a7 3-2A. An employer shall not contribute, except to a child account under \u00a7 3-3(d), shall not select or offer investments, shall not exercise any discretion over an account, and shall not be a plan sponsor, plan administrator, or fiduciary. An arrangement under this Act is not an employee benefit plan established or maintained by an employer within the meaning of the Employee Retirement Income Security Act of 1974, and nothing in this Act subjects an employer to that Act.\n\n**(d) State-administered touchpoints; funding condition only.** As a condition of Federal funding for a program administered by a State, the State shall furnish the notice under \u00a7 3-2A, and forward an indication of interest, to a person applying for or receiving a benefit under the Supplemental Nutrition Assistance Program, Medicaid, the Special Supplemental Nutrition Program for Women, Infants, and Children, or unemployment compensation. A State's role under this subsection is limited to furnishing a notice and forwarding an indication of interest. No State is required to enroll any person, to administer any account, or to perform any function of the Board or the Secretary, and no State is obligated to participate.\n\n**(e) Auto-escalation.** The default contribution rate increases by the increment set in the schedule to this Act on each anniversary of enrollment, to the maximum there set. An account holder may decline or cancel auto-escalation at any time by a single action, effective immediately.\n\n**(f) Additional channels.** In addition to the default channels, the Board and the Secretary shall make enrollment, contribution, and increase available through: an election on the Federal income-tax return; the direct diversion of all or part of a refund; electronic and mobile means, including a public application and website; and a simplified registration requiring only identity verification. These channels serve persons whom the defaults do not reach and persons who wish to contribute more than the default, and no channel is mandatory.\n\n**(g) Match visibility.** At the point of enrollment, contribution, or increase, the Board and the Secretary shall show the amount of match a contribution would earn."},{"anchor":"sec-3-2a","heading":"\u00a7 3-2A. Notice, Acknowledgment, and Comprehension","part":"SECTION 3 \u2014 THE PROSPERITY ACCOUNT","body":"**(a) Notice before the first contribution.** Before the first contribution is made on an individual's behalf, the Board shall furnish the individual a written notice stating: that an account has been created; that contributions will begin unless the individual declines; the amount and source of the default contribution; that at lower incomes the United States funds all or part of that contribution as a government share under \u00a7 3-6A, so that less or nothing is deducted from the individual's pay; that the money is the individual's own property; how to decline or opt out, in a single stated action; that opting out is effective immediately, requires no reason, and carries no penalty; that a contribution made within the preceding 90 days may be returned on request; and that an alternative non-interest-bearing option is available on request under \u00a7 3-1(h).\n\n**(b) Acknowledgment recorded.** The Board shall record the fact that the notice was furnished and, where the individual responds, the fact of the response. An individual is not required to consent, and enrollment is not conditioned on any response. This subsection requires a record that notice was given, not agreement to it.\n\n**(c) Comprehension tested and published.** The Board shall test the notice for comprehension among a representative sample of the population it addresses, before first use and not less than every three years thereafter, and shall publish the results, the questions used, and any revision made in consequence. Where fewer than the proportion set in the schedule to this Act answer the test questions correctly, the Board shall revise the notice and retest within 180 days, and shall publish each result. Enrollment under \u00a7 3-2 and the furnishing of notice under subsection (a) are unaffected by a test result.\n\n**(c-1) The test is not administered by the party it measures.** The Board shall publish the questions used, the sampling method, and the results disaggregated by the characteristics under which the sample was drawn, and shall engage a person independent of the Board to administer the test. A person is independent for this purpose who is not an officer or employee of the Board and whose compensation does not depend on the result of the test.\n\n**(d) Plain language and access.** The notice shall be written in plain language, furnished in the languages in which the Secretary furnishes tax forms, and made available in accessible formats."},{"anchor":"sec-3-3","heading":"\u00a7 3-3. Child Accounts, the Newborn Seed, and Absorption of Section 530A Accounts","part":"SECTION 3 \u2014 THE PROSPERITY ACCOUNT","body":"**(a) Child accounts.** A Prosperity Account may be held by a child under the age of 18, established and managed by a parent, guardian, or other authorized adult until the child attains the age of 18. A child account is available to any child holding a Social Security number or an individual taxpayer identification number; this eligibility is broader than, and supersedes, the citizenship requirement of section 530A of the Internal Revenue Code, and no child eligible under prior law loses eligibility under this Act.\n\n**(b) The newborn seed.** The Secretary shall deposit a one-time seed contribution, in the amount set in the schedule to this Act, into the Prosperity Account of each citizen child at birth or upon establishment of the account. This seed is permanent and universal among citizen children; it applies to every child who is a citizen of the United States and is not limited to any range of birth years. This subsection supersedes and replaces the pilot contribution program of section 6434 of the Internal Revenue Code, extending that program's one-time contribution to every citizen child on a permanent basis, and no child entitled to a contribution under section 6434 receives less under this Act. The seed under this subsection is available only to a citizen child; a child account may be held by a non-citizen child under subsection (a), but the federal seed is not deposited into it.\n\n**(c) Contribution limit.** Aggregate contributions to a child account from private sources \u2014 parents, guardians, family members, and employers \u2014 shall not exceed $5,000 per child per year, adjusted for inflation in increments of $100 for years after enactment. The newborn seed under subsection (b), the Prosperity Match under \u00a7 3-6, and qualified contributions by a government or tax-exempt organization do not count toward this limit and do not give rise to any excess-contribution excise tax under 26 U.S.C. \u00a7 4973 or otherwise.\n\n**(d) Employer contributions preserved.** An employer may contribute to the child account of an employee or an employee's dependent, up to the limit and under a program described in section 128 of the Internal Revenue Code. Such a contribution is excluded from the employee's gross income to the same extent as under section 128, which exclusion this Act preserves. Nothing in the merger of accounts under this Section reduces or repeals that exclusion.\n\n**(d-1) Contributions by engagers of independent workers.** A person who engages an individual as an independent contractor, or who operates a platform or marketplace through which an individual obtains work, may contribute to that individual's Prosperity Account, and to the account of that individual's dependent, on the same terms, subject to the same limits, and with the same exclusion from the individual's gross income as an employer contribution under subsection (d). A contribution under this subsection does not, by itself, establish an employment relationship or bear on the classification of the individual under any other law.\n\n**(e) Gift-tax treatment preserved.** A contribution to a child account qualifies for the annual gift-tax exclusion to the same extent, and under the same safe harbor, as a contribution to a section 530A account under Revenue Procedure 2026-25 and its successors. The merger of accounts under this Section does not alter that treatment.\n\n**(f) Absorption of section 530A accounts.** Each account established under section 530A of the Internal Revenue Code becomes a Prosperity Account under this Act, with no loss of value, no change adverse to the holder, and no interruption. A holder retains every contribution, seed, and benefit already received. The enrollment forms, contribution mechanisms, and electronic portal established for section 530A accounts serve as enrollment channels under \u00a7 3-2.\n\n**(g) No taxable event on absorption.** The absorption of a section 530A account into a Prosperity Account under subsection (f), and the continuation of a child account into a working-age account under subsection (h), are not a distribution, a rollover requiring inclusion, or any other realization event, and no amount is included in the gross income of any person by reason of either. A Prosperity Account holding amounts absorbed from a section 530A account retains individual-retirement-account treatment under section 408(a) of the Internal Revenue Code as to those amounts, so that tax-deferred growth and taxation on distribution are preserved and the holder's tax position is not made worse by the merger. The Secretary shall establish rules for tracing and ordering the amounts within a Prosperity Account that carry differing tax characters \u2014 including amounts carrying section 408(a) character, ordinary contributions, and federal match and seed \u2014 and for attributing growth among them, so that each distribution is taxed according to the character of the amounts distributed, in the manner of the basis-recovery and ordering rules of sections 72 and 408(d).\n\n**(h) Continuity into adulthood.** A child's Prosperity Account continues, upon the holder's attaining the age of 18, as the holder's working-age Prosperity Account, without conversion to a separate individual retirement account, without re-enrollment, and without any taxable event by reason of the transition. Before the age of 18, the account is subject to the distribution restrictions applicable to section 530A accounts as of enactment. Upon and after the age of 18, distributions are governed by the rules applicable to the working-age Prosperity Account under this Act and the individual-retirement-account rules of section 408, which continue to apply to amounts carrying that character. Where the holder is subject to a holding restriction under another Act, that restriction confines the account's investment menu but does not alter the section 408(a) tax character preserved under subsection (g)."},{"anchor":"sec-3-4","heading":"\u00a7 3-4. Funds","part":"SECTION 3 \u2014 THE PROSPERITY ACCOUNT","body":"**(a)** The Board shall offer the following funds and no others: a total United States equity fund; a global ex-United States equity fund; a United States small- and mid-capitalization completion fund; a broad investment-grade aggregate bond fund; an inflation-protected securities fund; the Government Securities (G) fund; lifecycle funds composed of the foregoing; and the non-interest-bearing alternative option required by \u00a7 3-1(h).\n\n**(b)** The Board shall not offer a sector, thematic, or single-industry fund; a leveraged or inverse fund; an options, futures, or commodities fund; a fund holding municipal, State, local, or Tribal government debt; a fund applying any screen, including an environmental, social, governance, or domestic-content screen; an actively managed or discretionary fund; or a fund holding digital assets.\n\n**(c)** Within each equity fund, holdings shall be weighted by global float-adjusted market capitalization. Within each fixed-income fund, holdings shall follow a formula set in the schedule to this Act. The lifecycle glidepath shall follow a formula set in the schedule to this Act.\n\n**(d)** Any amendment to subsection (a), (b), or (c) requires a recorded roll-call vote."},{"anchor":"sec-3-5","heading":"\u00a7 3-5. Governance of the Funds","part":"SECTION 3 \u2014 THE PROSPERITY ACCOUNT","body":"**(a) Index-only.** Each fund shall track a published index. No fund shall be actively managed or apply any screen.\n\n**(b) No directed investment.** No person shall direct the investment of a fund toward or away from any issuer, sector, or geography. A violation is subject to civil penalty and removal of the responsible person by the Board, and, where the violator is a covered person under the Executive Integrity Act or the Congressional Integrity Act, to the criminal provisions of the applicable Act.\n\n**(c) Voting.** The Board shall vote all proxies in proportion to the votes cast by all other shareholders, or shall pass through voting to beneficiaries. No fund shall exercise independent influence over the governance of any issuer.\n\n**(c-1) Mirror voting absolute as to defaulted contributions.** With respect to shares attributable to a contribution made by default under \u00a7 3-2, the Board shall vote strictly in proportion to the votes cast by all other shareholders on the same question, shall exercise no independent judgment on any question presented to shareholders, and shall not withhold a vote, abstain as a matter of judgment, support or oppose any proposal, or communicate any view to any issuer. This requirement is absolute and is not waivable by the Board, by any officer of the United States, or by rule.\n\n**(c-2) How the proportional vote is cast.** Because the votes cast by other shareholders are not known until the poll closes, the Board shall discharge subsection (c-1) by instructing the tabulator to allocate its shares in proportion to the final tally of all other shares voted on the question, or by voting after that tally is available. Where neither method is available on a question, the Board shall not vote the shares.\n\n**(d) Ownership cap.** The Board shall not hold, in any fund, more than a percentage of any single issuer set in the schedule to this Act. Shares above the cap shall be held in non-voting form.\n\n**(e) Board independence.** The members of the Board shall serve staggered terms and may be removed only for cause.\n\n**(f) Corpus protection.** The assets of the accounts and funds under this Section are held in trust for the account holders, are not the property of the United States, and shall not be borrowed against, appropriated, or diverted to any purpose other than the benefit of the account holders."},{"anchor":"sec-3-6","heading":"\u00a7 3-6. The Prosperity Match","part":"SECTION 3 \u2014 THE PROSPERITY ACCOUNT","body":"**(a)** The Secretary of the Treasury shall deposit a matching contribution into the Prosperity Account of each eligible individual who is a citizen of the United States and who contributes to that account. The match is refundable, is not conditioned on income-tax liability, and is deposited directly. A non-citizen may hold and contribute to a Prosperity Account under this Section but does not receive the federal match or the seed under subsection (d). For purposes of this Section, a contribution by the individual does not include the government share of the default contribution under \u00a7 3-6A, which does not earn the match.\n\n**(b)** The match rate is highest at the lowest incomes and declines continuously to zero at approximately the 80th percentile of the income distribution. The schedule to this Act sets the rate.\n\n**(b-1) Wealth adjustment.** The match rate is reduced, on the schedule to this Act, for an individual whose reportable wealth exceeds the wealth threshold set in that schedule. The threshold shall be set high enough that the adjustment reaches only individuals whose assets place them well above the income at which the match has already declined to zero.\n\n**(b-2) No individual is asked about assets unless the individual's own return already indicates the individual may hold them.** The duty to report under subsection (b-3) arises only for an individual whose return for the year required a schedule reporting interest and dividends above the amount set in the schedule to this Act, business income, capital gains, rents, royalties, partnership or S corporation income, or farm income. **An individual whose return required none of those has no duty under this Section, is asked nothing, and enrollment under \u00a7 3-2 is unaffected.**\n\n**(b-3) What is reported.** An individual described in subsection (b-2) shall report, with the individual's return, the fair market value of \u2014 real property other than the individual's principal residence; a closely held business interest; an interest in a partnership, trust, or private fund; and financial assets not reported on an information return. **No other class of asset is reportable, and the omission of a class is a determination by Congress and not a gap.**\n\n**(b-4) The principal residence is excluded.** The individual's principal residence, and the debt secured by it, are not reportable wealth.\n\n**(b-5) The consequence is confined to the match.** A failure to report under subsection (b-3) reduces or eliminates the match for that year and has no other effect. **The account, automatic enrollment, the newborn seed, the government share of the default contribution, and every protection under \u00a7 3-9 and \u00a7 3-10 are unaffected**, and no account may be closed, frozen, or made ineligible by reason of this Section.\n\n**(b-6) Misreporting carries the consequence it already carries.** A report under subsection (b-3) is made on the return under penalty of perjury and is subject to the ordinary provisions of the Internal Revenue Code governing a false statement on a return. **This Section creates no new penalty, no new examination authority, and no new reporting obligation for any third party.**\n\n**(c)** The match applies a higher rate to a lower contribution cap, such that the first dollars contributed are matched at the highest rate. The match applies only to the first $7,500 of contributions in a year, adjusted for inflation from the date of enactment.\n\n**(d)** Each eligible individual who is a citizen of the United States and who opens and funds a Prosperity Account with any amount receives a fixed refundable seed contribution, set in the schedule to this Act, regardless of the amount contributed. This seed is in addition to the newborn seed under \u00a7 3-3(b).\n\n**(e)** A match and seed under this Section are deposited at the expense ceiling under \u00a7 3-1(f).\n\n**(f) Tax treatment of a match, a seed, and the government share.** A matching contribution under subsection (a), a seed under subsection (d), the newborn seed under \u00a7 3-3(b), and a government share of the default contribution under \u00a7 3-6A are not includable in the gross income of the account holder, of a parent or guardian of a child account holder, or of any other person, in the year of deposit or in any later year by reason of the deposit. Each is a refundable credit paid directly into the account. This subsection governs the deposit only: a distribution from the account is taxed according to the character of the amounts distributed under the tracing and ordering rules of \u00a7 3-3(g), and nothing in this subsection exempts a distribution, or growth attributable to a match, seed, or government share, from inclusion under those rules."},{"anchor":"sec-3-6a","heading":"\u00a7 3-6A. Government Share of the Default Contribution","part":"SECTION 3 \u2014 THE PROSPERITY ACCOUNT","body":"**(a) Purpose.** A default contribution deducted from the paycheck of a household living at its margin is a real cost, and the household most in need of the account is the household least able to absorb the deduction. This Section removes that cost at the lowest incomes without reducing the contribution the account receives: at those incomes the United States funds the default contribution, in whole or in part, so that the account is funded while the paycheck is not diminished. The default contribution is thereby preserved at full value for the households this Act exists to serve, and the incentive to opt out that a paycheck deduction creates is removed rather than managed.\n\n**(a-1) Adequacy.** The schedule to this Act shall set the default contribution rate under \u00a7 3-2(a)(1) and the maximum government share under subsection (b) so that an eligible individual who never contributes beyond the default, and for whom the government share applies at its maximum throughout, accumulates by retirement a real balance not less than the amount stated in the schedule, calculated using the return and wage-growth assumptions stated in the schedule. This subsection binds the setting of the schedule and creates no individual entitlement to any particular balance.\n\n**(b) The government share.** For an eligible individual who is a citizen of the United States and who is enrolled through payroll deduction under \u00a7 3-2(a)(1), the Secretary of the Treasury shall deposit into the individual's Prosperity Account a government share of the default contribution. The government share is a percentage of the default contribution that is highest at the lowest incomes \u2014 up to the whole of the default contribution, so that no amount is deducted from the paycheck \u2014 and declines continuously to zero at approximately the 40th percentile of the income distribution. The schedule to this Act sets the maximum share and the phase-out. Above the income at which the government share reaches zero, the individual funds the whole default contribution, as under \u00a7 3-2(a)(1).\n\n**(c) Worker's share withheld.** The portion of the default contribution not funded by the government share under subsection (b) is the worker's share, and is the amount deducted from the individual's pay and forwarded by the employer under \u00a7 3-2(c). Where the government share is the whole of the default contribution, the worker's share is zero and no amount is deducted. An employer's obligation is limited, as under \u00a7 3-2(c), to forwarding the worker's share as determined and communicated by the Secretary; the employer neither computes the government share nor funds any part of it.\n\n**(d) Real-time determination and reconciliation.** The Secretary shall determine each individual's worker's share prospectively, from the best available current information as to the individual's income, and shall reconcile the government share against the individual's income for the year when that income is known, in the manner of the reconciliation of an advance payment of a refundable credit. An excess government share attributable to an under-estimate of income is recovered by reducing a later government share or a later match, and not by any deduction from the paycheck or any charge against the balance already in the account. A shortfall is deposited on reconciliation. No penalty attaches to an individual for a difference between the prospective and the reconciled share.\n\n**(d-1) No communication, no worker's share.** Where no worker's share has been communicated to the employer for a pay period, the worker's share for that period is zero and the government share is the whole of the default contribution, subject to reconciliation under subsection (d). Non-delivery of a determination is a cost borne by the United States and not by the worker.\n\n**(d-2) The determination is annual.** The prospective determination under subsection (d) is made annually, from the individual's most recent return or from information returns, and is redetermined during the year on the individual's request. Nothing in this Section requires a determination to be made or communicated for each pay period.\n\n**(d-3) Communicated through machinery that exists.** The Secretary shall communicate the worker's share through the existing wage-withholding mechanism, and no employer is required to receive it by any other means. The Secretary shall establish no new channel of communication to employers for this purpose.\n\n**(e) Not a matchable contribution.** The government share under this Section is not a contribution by the individual and does not earn the Prosperity Match under \u00a7 3-6. Only the worker's share, and any amount the individual contributes above the default, is a contribution of the individual for purposes of \u00a7 3-6.\n\n**(f) Character of the government share.** A government share deposited under this Section is the property of the account holder from the moment it is deposited, as provided in \u00a7 3-1(d-1); is a contribution made by default under \u00a7 3-2 for purposes of the absolute mirror-voting rule of \u00a7 3-5(c-1); and is deposited at the expense ceiling under \u00a7 3-1(f). Because the government share is not the individual's earned income, it does not enter the computation of any earned income tax credit or other income-tested benefit, and the after-tax character of the default contribution under \u00a7 3-1(g) is unaffected.\n\n**(g) Opt-out.** An individual for whom a government share is deposited retains the right to opt out under \u00a7 3-1(d) on the same terms as any other individual. On an opt-out, a government share deposited within the return window under \u00a7 3-1(d) is recovered from the account, as a match on a returned contribution is recovered under that subsection. Declining the account forgoes the government share.\n\n**(h) Relationship to the Match.** The government share and the Prosperity Match are distinct instruments serving distinct purposes. The government share relieves the cash-flow burden of the default contribution at the lowest incomes and phases out at approximately the 40th percentile; the Match rewards contribution and phases out at approximately the 80th. An individual below the phase-out of the government share receives both: the United States funds the default contribution, and the Match is paid on the worker's share and on any amount contributed above the default."},{"anchor":"sec-3-7","heading":"\u00a7 3-7. Transparency and Durability","part":"SECTION 3 \u2014 THE PROSPERITY ACCOUNT","body":"**(a) Named, visible account.** The account shall be identified to the holder by a consistent name, and the Board shall provide each holder a regular statement of the holder's balance, contributions, match, and growth, so that the benefit is visible to the holder.\n\n**(b) Take-up reporting.** The Board shall publish annually, by income decile and by enrollment channel: the rate of enrollment, the rate of opt-out, the rate of contribution, average and median balances, and the distribution of the match paid. The purpose of this subsection is to determine whether the match is reaching the households for which it was designed or is concentrating among households that would have saved without it, which is the failure mode of a matching subsidy offered on an elective basis. Where the published data show such concentration, the Board shall so state in the report.\n\n**(c) Annual report.** The Board shall publish an annual public report on the operation and reach of the system, including the number of accounts, contributions, match paid, and seeds paid.\n\n**(d) No phase-in by cohort.** The Prosperity Match under \u00a7 3-6(a), the seed under \u00a7 3-6(d), and the newborn seed under \u00a7 3-3(b) accrue to every individual eligible for them from the date on which Section 3 takes effect under \u00a7 4-1(b). No individual is brought into the system by cohort, step, or class, and no match or seed is deferred by reason of the date on which the Secretary is able to deposit it. The Secretary shall deposit a match or seed that has accrued and has not been deposited in full, without application, not later than the end of the calendar year following the year in which it accrued. The Secretary shall give priority in outreach and enrollment to lower-income and previously-unbanked persons; that priority orders the Secretary's work and does not defer any person's match or seed. The government share under \u00a7 3-6A is governed by \u00a7 4-1(c)."},{"anchor":"sec-3-8","heading":"\u00a7 3-8. Education Accounts","part":"SECTION 3 \u2014 THE PROSPERITY ACCOUNT","body":"A participant may elect a fund under \u00a7 3-4 as the investment option within a State qualified tuition program that permits it. This Act does not establish a federal qualified tuition program."},{"anchor":"sec-3-9","heading":"\u00a7 3-9. Treatment of Accounts Under Other Federal Programs","part":"SECTION 3 \u2014 THE PROSPERITY ACCOUNT","body":"This Section applies to every Prosperity Account, whether held by a child or an adult.\n\n**(a) Financial-aid neutrality.** A Prosperity Account, and the assets in it, shall not be treated as an asset of the student or of the parent in determining eligibility for Federal need-based student financial aid, and shall be disregarded in the calculation of expected family contribution or student aid index.\n\n**(b) Means-tested benefit neutrality.** A Prosperity Account, the assets in it, and its earnings are disregarded in determining eligibility for, or the amount of, any Federal means-tested program, and any State program administered with Federal funds, as follows:\n\n1. **Health coverage and non-cash programs.** The account and its assets are disregarded without limitation in determining eligibility for Medicaid, the Children's Health Insurance Program, the Supplemental Nutrition Assistance Program, and Federally assisted housing.\n\n2. **Cash assistance.** For the purpose of a Federal means-tested cash benefit, including Supplemental Security Income, the account and its assets are disregarded without limitation.\n\n3. **No cliff.** No cash benefit described in paragraph (2) is suspended, reduced, or terminated by reason of the balance of a Prosperity Account, however large, and no beneficiary is required to report that balance for the purpose of that benefit.\n\n4. **No State asset test.** As a condition of receiving Federal funds for a program described in this subsection, a State shall not count a Prosperity Account against an asset or resource limit in that program. This paragraph operates as a condition on the receipt of Federal funds and does not direct a State in the exercise of its own authority. This paragraph applies to determinations made on or after the first full fiscal year beginning two years after enactment.\n\n5. **No recovery at death.** No Federal or State program shall claim reimbursement from a Prosperity Account, and no lien or recovery shall attach to it, by reason of benefits paid to the account holder. The balance passes as the account holder directs or as law otherwise provides.\n\n**(b-1) The disregard operates as a matter of Federal law.** A Prosperity Account, the assets in it, and its earnings are disregarded as a matter of Federal law in determining eligibility for, or the amount of, any program described in subsection (b), whether or not a State has amended its State plan, its rules, or its eligibility systems. The condition in paragraph (b)(4) is a backstop to this subsection and not the mechanism by which it operates.\n\n**(b-2) Long-term services and supports.** For the purpose of the long-term services and supports subsidy under the Long-Term Care Act, a Prosperity Account, the assets in it, and its earnings are disregarded to the extent the balance does not exceed the amount set in the schedule to this Act. A balance in excess of that amount is a countable resource under \u00a7 33-3 of that Act, and adjusts the subsidy by the continuous factor that section prescribes. Paragraph (b)(3) does not apply to the subsidy, which is neither suspended nor terminated by reason of an account balance, and nothing in this subsection permits a spend-down, a resource cliff, or a recovery prohibited by paragraph (b)(5) or by \u00a7 33-3(c) of that Act."},{"anchor":"sec-3-10","heading":"\u00a7 3-10. Access, Hardship, and Protection of the Account","part":"SECTION 3 \u2014 THE PROSPERITY ACCOUNT","body":"**(a) Purpose.** A person without savings will not lock away money that cannot be reached in an emergency. This Section provides a defined route to the account in hardship, and protects the account from seizure, so that saving is a rational choice for a household living close to its margin.\n\n**(b) Hardship withdrawal.** An account holder aged 18 or older may withdraw from the working-age Prosperity Account, without penalty under this Act, an amount necessary to meet an immediate and heavy financial need, including: unreimbursed medical expenses; the prevention of eviction, foreclosure, or utility disconnection; the repair or replacement of a primary residence or of a vehicle necessary to obtain or keep work; funeral expenses; tuition and related educational expenses; and expenses arising from a federally declared disaster or from the loss of employment.\n\n**(c) Administration of hardship withdrawal.** The Board shall administer subsection (b) on the account holder's certification of the need and its amount, without requiring documentation in advance, subject to later verification. The Board shall not condition a hardship withdrawal on exhausting other resources, and shall complete a hardship withdrawal promptly. A withdrawal under subsection (b) is included in gross income to the extent it consists of amounts not previously taxed, determined under the tracing and ordering rules of \u00a7 3-3(g), and is not subject to any additional tax or penalty imposed by reason of early distribution.\n\n**(d) Contributions may resume.** A hardship withdrawal does not suspend eligibility to contribute, to receive the Prosperity Match, or to receive any seed under this Act.\n\n**(e) Protection from creditors.** A Prosperity Account, its assets, and its earnings are exempt from levy, attachment, garnishment, execution, and any other legal or equitable process for the collection of a debt, and are exempt property in bankruptcy, to the same extent as a retirement plan qualified under section 401(a) of the Internal Revenue Code. A purported assignment, pledge, or grant of a security interest in a Prosperity Account is void; a loan under \u00a7 3-11, which creates no security interest, is not an assignment, pledge, or grant of a security interest for purposes of this subsection.\n\n**(f) Exceptions to protection.** Subsection (e) does not apply to: a domestic support obligation, including child support and spousal support; a Federal tax lien or levy; an order of criminal restitution or forfeiture; or a disgorgement or penalty owed under the Executive Integrity Act, the Congressional Integrity Act, the Judiciary Integrity Act, or the Candidate Integrity Act. The purpose of subsection (e) is to protect household savings from consumer debt collection, not to shield a person from an obligation to a child, a victim, or the public. **These exceptions are exclusive. No other exception applies, whether arising in equity, at common law, or by implication from any other statute, and the omission of an exception is a determination by Congress and not a gap.**\n\n**(g) Anti-predation.** No person shall condition the extension of credit, the sale of goods or services, or the provision of employment on an account holder's withdrawal from, assignment of, or grant of an interest in a Prosperity Account. A violation is subject to civil penalty and the transaction is voidable by the account holder.\n\n**(g-1) Enforcement.** An account holder against whom subsection (g) is violated **may bring a civil action** in a district court of the United States, or in any State court of competent jurisdiction, **to avoid the transaction, to recover actual damages and the civil penalty, and for injunctive relief**, and **the court shall award a prevailing account holder reasonable attorney's fees and costs.** **The right and the action are conferred expressly by this subsection; no inference is required to establish either, and no presumption against the creation of a private right or a private remedy applies.** The Secretary may also bring an action to enforce subsection (g); **no action or inaction by the Secretary is a condition of, or a bar to, an action by an account holder.**"},{"anchor":"sec-3-11","heading":"\u00a7 3-11. Prosperity Account Loans","part":"SECTION 3 \u2014 THE PROSPERITY ACCOUNT","body":"**(a) Loan availability.** An account holder whose account balance exceeds a minimum threshold set in the schedule may borrow against the account balance.\n\n**(a-1) Relation to the individual-retirement-account rules.** A loan made under this Section by the Board, on the terms of subsections (b) through (i) as those terms stood when the loan was made \u2014\n\n1. is not a prohibited transaction under section 4975 of the Internal Revenue Code;\n\n2. is not a use of the account, or of any portion of it, as security for a loan within the meaning of section 408(e)(4) of that Code;\n\n3. does not disqualify the account, and does not cause any amount held in it to be treated as distributed, under section 408(e)(2) of that Code; and\n\n4. is not subject to any other provision of that Code that, by reason of a loan, would treat an amount held in an individual retirement account as distributed or would terminate the account's treatment under section 408(a).\n\nThis subsection applies whether or not the account holds amounts carrying section 408(a) character under \u00a7 3-3(g), and applies to those amounts.\n\n**(a-2) Scope and limits of subsection (a-1).** Compliance with subsections (b) through (i) is determined as of the date the loan is made, and a later change in the account balance, in the account holder's employment, or in any other circumstance does not withdraw the treatment provided by subsection (a-1). An error by the Board in administering this Section is not chargeable to the account holder and does not withdraw that treatment. An extension of credit against a Prosperity Account that is not made under this Section receives no treatment under subsection (a-1). Where a loan is in default under subsection (h), the consequence is the distribution treatment provided in that subsection and no other; a default is not a prohibited transaction and does not disqualify the account.\n\n**(b) Loan amount.** Total outstanding loan balances may not exceed 50 percent of the account balance at the time any new loan is taken.\n\n**(b-1) Proportional draw.** A loan is drawn proportionally from the account holder's investment allocation at the time of the loan. Repayment is credited proportionally to the allocation in effect at the time of each repayment.\n\n**(c) Interest rate.** The interest rate is the G Fund rate in effect at the time of the loan. Interest paid is credited to the borrower's own account.\n\n**(c-1) Tax treatment of interest.** Interest paid on a loan under this Section is not deductible. Interest credited to the account is treated as an after-tax contribution for purposes of determining the tax character of subsequent withdrawals.\n\n**(d) No credit check.** A loan under this Section is not conditioned on credit history, credit score, or income verification. No asset of the account holder other than the account balance may be reached to satisfy a loan under this Section, and no security interest in the account is created, granted, or required.\n\n**(e) Repayment as contribution.** While any loan balance is outstanding:\n\n1. All amounts that would otherwise be contributed through payroll deduction under \u00a7 3-2(a)(1) or refund diversion under \u00a7 3-2(a)(2) are applied to repayment of the loan principal and accrued interest.\n\n2. The account holder may contribute above the amounts applied to loan repayment. A contribution so made is applied first to repayment of the loan and thereafter to the account. No account holder is barred from saving by reason of an outstanding loan.\n\n3. For purposes of this Act, amounts applied to loan repayment are treated as contributions for purposes of the federal match under \u00a7 3-6 and the government share under \u00a7 3-6A. This treatment applies for purposes of this Act only and does not affect the characterization of loan repayment for any other purpose under Federal law.\n\n**(f) Resumption after repayment.** Upon full repayment of all outstanding loans, contributions under \u00a7 3-2 are applied wholly to the account, with no gap or penalty, and no re-enrollment or other action by the account holder is required.\n\n**(g) Repayment.** Repayment occurs through the application of withholding under subsection (e) until the loan balance is zero. There is no maximum term and no minimum payment.\n\n**(h) Default.** A loan is in default if no repayment has been received through any channel, including the refund diversion applied under subsection (h-1), for 90 consecutive days. The 90-day period is tolled for any period during which the account holder has no wages from which a deduction under \u00a7 3-2(a)(1) could be made, until the earlier of the next refund diversion under \u00a7 3-2(a)(2) or 12 months from the start of the tolling. A loan in default is treated as a distribution to the extent of the unpaid principal and accrued interest as of the date of default, subject to \u00a7 3-10 (hardship exceptions and tax treatment). The taxable event occurs on the date of default. The Board shall provide notice before default treatment and shall offer a hardship extension where circumstances warrant.\n\n**(h-1) Continuity on separation from employment.** Upon an account holder's separation from employment, the Board shall automatically apply refund diversion under \u00a7 3-2(a)(2) to the repayment of any outstanding loan under this Section, without requiring any election or other action by the account holder. The account holder may instead arrange an alternative repayment method, or may opt out of continued repayment, at any time. This subsection does not extend the 90-day period under subsection (h).\n\n**(i) Fee cap.** The Board may charge an administrative fee not exceeding an amount set in the schedule. No origination fee, prepayment penalty, or other charge may be imposed.\n\n**(j) Loan offered first.** When an account holder requests a hardship withdrawal under \u00a7 3-10 and the requested amount is within the loan limit under this Section, the Board shall:\n\n1. Present the loan option under this Section as the default;\n\n2. Display a side-by-side comparison showing projected account balance at retirement under each option, using reasonable assumptions disclosed on the comparison;\n\n3. Require the account holder to affirmatively select the hardship withdrawal if they prefer it after viewing the comparison.\n\nThe account holder may proceed with the withdrawal after viewing the comparison; this subsection creates no barrier, only informed choice.\n\n**(k) Aggregate reporting.** The Board shall publish annually aggregate statistics on loans under this Section, including the number and dollar amount of loans outstanding, the utilization rate as a share of eligible account holders, the incidence of default under subsection (h), and the aggregate effect of outstanding loans on projected account balances at retirement, using the assumptions disclosed under subsection (j); **and the time from a request for a loan under this Section, and from a hardship withdrawal request under \u00a7 3-10, to the disbursement of funds, at the median and at the ninetieth percentile.** **Where those times exceed the periods set in the schedule in more than the share set in the schedule, that is evidence that the staffing of the functions described in \u00a7 4-2(f) is inadequate and shall be stated as such**, together with **the personnel required to meet them**, transmitted to the Congress and renewed annually until they are met.\n\n**(k-1) Servicing rides on existing channels.** The Board shall administer repayment under this Section through the channels established under \u00a7 3-2 and shall establish no separate collection mechanism."},{"anchor":"sec-4-1","heading":"\u00a7 4-1. Effective Dates","part":"SECTION 4 \u2014 GENERAL PROVISIONS","body":"**(a)** Section 1 takes effect one year after enactment, to permit the Bureau of Labor Statistics to establish the series required.\n\n**(b)** Sections 2 and 3 take effect one year after enactment, except that \u00a7 3-3(f), absorbing accounts established under section 530A of the Internal Revenue Code, takes effect upon enactment and no such account is interrupted.\n\n**(c) The government share.** Notwithstanding subsection (b), \u00a7 3-6A applies from the second full calendar year beginning after enactment. Until it applies, the government share is paid as a credit on reconciliation under \u00a7 3-6A(d) and the worker's share is the whole of the default contribution. The entitlement to the government share is not deferred by this subsection; only the prospective per-worker determination is.","partHeads":[{"heading":"SECTION 4 \u2014 GENERAL PROVISIONS","body":""}]},{"anchor":"sec-4-2","heading":"\u00a7 4-2. Resources and Continuity of Operations","part":"SECTION 4 \u2014 GENERAL PROVISIONS","body":"**(a) Permanent appropriation for the match and the seeds.** There are appropriated, out of any money in the Treasury not otherwise appropriated, such sums as are necessary to pay the Prosperity Match under \u00a7 3-6, the government share of the default contribution under \u00a7 3-6A, the seed under \u00a7 3-6(d), and the newborn seed under \u00a7 3-3(b). Amounts under this subsection are appropriated permanently and indefinitely, are available without further appropriation and without fiscal-year limitation, and do not lapse.\n\n**(b) Authorization for administration.** There are authorized to be appropriated, for each fiscal year, such sums as are necessary for the administration of this Act, including the statistical work required by Section 1 and funded under \u00a7 1-6; the administration of the Fund; the pooled recordkeeping and account administration required by \u00a7 3-1(f); the enrollment systems and channels required by \u00a7 3-2; the notice, acknowledgment, and comprehension testing required by \u00a7 3-2A; the alternative option required by \u00a7 3-1(h); and the publications required by \u00a7 3-7.\n\n**(c) No-year availability.** Amounts appropriated under subsection (b) are available until expended.\n\n**(d) No obligation without appropriation.** No **administrative** duty imposed by this Act \u2014 that is, a duty for which subsection (b) authorizes appropriations \u2014 is effective in a fiscal year except to the extent that amounts are appropriated to carry it out. Where amounts are insufficient, the Board and the Secretary shall publish a statement identifying each duty not performed and the amount required, and shall transmit it to the Congress. No penalty or consequence attaches to any agency, officer, or employee for a duty not performed for want of appropriation. This subsection does not apply to subsection (a), which is permanently appropriated; **to a series funded by \u00a7 1-6(a-1)**; **or to subsection (e).**\n\n**(e) No displacement.** Amounts appropriated under this Act are in addition to, and shall not be derived from, amounts otherwise available to the Board, the Bureau of Labor Statistics, or the Department of the Treasury for other purposes.\n\n**(f) Continuity during a lapse in appropriations.** The following are activities authorized by law to continue during a lapse in appropriations, and obligations incurred for them are authorized in advance of appropriations for purposes of section 1342 of title 31, United States Code: the payment of the match, the government share under \u00a7 3-6A, and the seeds under subsection (a); the receipt and crediting of contributions to Prosperity Accounts; the maintenance of account balances and the execution of investment elections; the processing of a withdrawal, including a hardship withdrawal under \u00a7 3-10; the processing of an opt-out under \u00a7 3-1(d) and the return of contributions it requires; the creation of accounts and deposit of newborn seeds under \u00a7 3-3; and the certification of the Middle-Class Growth Rate under \u00a7 1-3 on its published date.\n\n**(g) Excepted personnel.** The officers and employees necessary to perform the activities in subsection (f) shall be designated as excepted from furlough during a lapse in appropriations. The Board, the Secretary, and the Bureau shall identify those positions in their shutdown plans, publish the identification, and maintain staffing sufficient to perform the activities without interruption.\n\n**(h) Account assets are not affected by a lapse.** The assets held in Prosperity Accounts are held in trust for the account holders, are not the property of the United States, and are not available for any purpose of the United States. A lapse in appropriations does not suspend, reduce, or impair any account holder's interest, and does not prevent a holder from withdrawing, contributing, or changing an investment election.\n\n**(i) Deadlines.** A deadline imposed by this Act on an account holder, an employer, or any other private party is tolled for the duration of a lapse in appropriations affecting the agency concerned, and for 14 days thereafter."},{"anchor":"sec-4-3","heading":"\u00a7 4-3. Amendment","part":"SECTION 4 \u2014 GENERAL PROVISIONS","body":"An amendment to the fund menu under \u00a7 3-4, to the allocation formulas under \u00a7 3-4(c), to the Middle-Class Growth Rate, to the Prosperity Match under \u00a7 3-6, or to the government share of the default contribution under \u00a7 3-6A requires a recorded roll-call vote. The methodology lock of \u00a7 1-2 governs amendment of the Middle-Class Growth Rate."},{"anchor":"sec-4-4","heading":"\u00a7 4-4. Severability","part":"SECTION 4 \u2014 GENERAL PROVISIONS","body":"If any provision of this Act, or its application to any person or circumstance, is held invalid, the remainder of this Act and its application to other persons or circumstances are not affected. Each Section is severable from every other.\n\n**(a) Independence from the integrity Acts.** No provision of this Act depends for its operation or validity on the enactment, validity, or continued effect of the Executive Integrity Act, the Congressional Integrity Act, the Judiciary Integrity Act, or the Candidate Integrity Act. If any of those Acts is held invalid in whole or in part, or is not enacted, or is repealed, this Act remains in full force. Where a provision of this Act refers to a body established by another Act, and that body does not exist or cannot lawfully act, the function is performed by the Secretary or the Secretary's designee.\n\n**(b) Each program severable.** Section 1 (the Middle-Class Growth Rate), Section 2 (the Fund), and Section 3 (the Prosperity Account) are each severable from the others and operate independently. The Fund under Section 2 is not among the funds offered under \u00a7 3-4 and is not an investment option in a Prosperity Account. If Section 2 is held invalid or the Fund is not issued, the Middle-Class Growth Rate continues to be published under Section 1 and the Prosperity Account continues to operate under Section 3 without any change.\n\n**(c) Protections severable.** The benefit- and aid-neutrality provisions of \u00a7 3-9 and the hardship-access and creditor-protection provisions of \u00a7 3-10 are severable from one another and from every other provision, and each paragraph of each is severable.\n\n**(d) Enrollment severable to opt-in.** If enrollment by default under \u00a7 3-2 is held invalid, in whole or as to any channel or any class of person, the account, the seed, the match, the fund menu, and every other provision of this Act continue in force, and enrollment proceeds through the channels of \u00a7 3-2(f) on an elective basis for the class as to which the default is invalid. An adverse holding as to the default narrows the manner of enrollment and does not affect the program.\n\n**(e) Channels severable from one another.** The payroll channel of \u00a7 3-2(a)(1), the refund channel of \u00a7 3-2(a)(2), the account-and-seed channel of \u00a7 3-2(a)(3), and the State touchpoint condition of \u00a7 3-2(d) are each severable from the others. If the State touchpoint condition is held invalid, including on the ground that it directs a State, the Federal mechanism of \u00a7 3-2(b) continues and is sufficient without it.\n\n**(f) Accommodations severable and preserved.** The alternative option of \u00a7 3-1(h), the absolute mirror-voting rule of \u00a7 3-5(c-1), the notice and acknowledgment provisions of \u00a7 3-2A, and the after-tax default of \u00a7 3-1(g) are severable from every other provision. If enrollment by default is held invalid, these provisions continue to apply to enrollment on any basis. None of them may be severed in a manner that leaves enrollment by default in force without them."},{"anchor":"schedule","heading":"SCHEDULE","part":null,"body":"1. **Appropriation to the Bureau of Labor Statistics** (\u00a7 1-6): [such sums as necessary, including CPS sample expansion; the Bureau's annual adequacy certification under \u00a7 1-6(f) states the required amount]\n2. *(Reserved.)*\n3. **Expense ceiling** (\u00a7 3-1(f)): [___ basis points]\n4. **Equity/fixed-income and glidepath formulas** (\u00a7 3-4(c)): [___]\n5. **Per-issuer ownership cap** (\u00a7 3-5(d)): [___%]\n6. **Prosperity Match rate schedule** (\u00a7 3-6(b)): [___]\n7. **Prosperity Match contribution cap and rate tiers** (\u00a7 3-6(c)): [___]\n8. **Refundable seed amount** (\u00a7 3-6(d)): [$___]\n9. **Default payroll contribution rate** (\u00a7 3-2(a)(1)): [3% of compensation]\n10. **Maximum government share of the default contribution** (\u00a7 3-6A(b)): [up to 100% of the default contribution at the lowest incomes, so that no amount is deducted from the paycheck]\n11. **Phase-out of the government share** (\u00a7 3-6A(b)): [government share declining continuously to zero at approximately the 40th percentile of the income distribution]\n12. **Default refund diversion share** (\u00a7 3-2(a)(2)): [10% of any refund]\n13. **Auto-escalation increment and maximum** (\u00a7 3-2(e)): [1 percentage point per year, to a maximum of 8%]\n14. **Contribution return window after opt-out** (\u00a7 3-1(d)): [90 days]\n15. **Newborn seed amount** (\u00a7 3-3(b)): [$___; may not be set below $1,000 for children born 2025\u20132028]\n16. **Minimum account balance to borrow** (\u00a7 3-11(a)): [$1,000]\n17. **Loan administrative fee cap** (\u00a7 3-11(i)): [$50]\n18. **Minimum real retirement accumulation target for a lifetime non-contributor, and the return and wage-growth assumptions used to calculate it** (\u00a7 3-6A(a-1)): [$___, stated in dollars adjusted for inflation from the date of enactment]\n19. **Term of the Commissioner of Labor Statistics** (\u00a7 1-5A(b)): [4 years]\n20. **Period to respond to a statement of grounds for removal** (\u00a7 1-5A(c)(2)): [___ days]\n21. **Period after a release within which an adverse action against the responsible official requires a published statement of grounds** (\u00a7 1-5A(e)): [___ days]\n22. **Proportion of a comprehension-test sample answering correctly, below which the notice must be revised and retested** (\u00a7 3-2A(c)): [___%]\n23. **Periods from a \u00a7 3-11 loan request and a \u00a7 3-10 hardship-withdrawal request to disbursement, and the share exceeded constituting a shortfall** (\u00a7 3-11(k)): [___ days; ___ percent]\n24. **Wealth threshold, and the reduction of the Prosperity Match rate for reportable wealth above it** (\u00a7 3-6(b-1)): [$___; reduction schedule ___]\n25. **Amount of interest and dividends above which a return gives rise to the duty to report wealth** (\u00a7 3-6(b-2)): [$___]"}],"asOf":"2026-11-16"}}}};
